<a id="challenge-mqtt-exit13"></a>

# 11. Challenge â€” Mosquitto Container Exit Code 13
## Symptom

The MQTT container failed to remain running.

Logs reported:

```text
Unable to open log file /mosquitto/log/mosquitto.log
```

and:

```text
Unable to open pwfile /mosquitto/config/passwordfile
```

The container exited with:

```text
exit code 13
```

## Initial Investigation

The configuration appeared structurally correct.

The Compose mounts existed:

```text
../mosquitto/config:/mosquitto/config
../mosquitto/data:/mosquitto/data
../mosquitto/log:/mosquitto/log
```

The problem therefore moved toward host filesystem access.

---

<a id="challenge-mqtt-permissions"></a>

# 12. Challenge â€” Mosquitto Bind-Mount Permissions
## Root Cause

The Mosquitto process inside the container did not have permission to access the mounted host files.

The container runs using a Mosquitto service identity.

The HomeLab ultimately aligned the mounted directories with:

```text
UID/GID 1883:1883
```

## Resolution

Correct ownership:

```bash
sudo chown -R 1883:1883 \
  ~/docker/mosquitto
```

Directories:

```bash
sudo find ~/docker/mosquitto \
  -type d \
  -exec chmod 755 {} \;
```

Normal files:

```bash
sudo find ~/docker/mosquitto \
  -type f \
  ! -name passwordfile \
  -exec chmod 644 {} \;
```

Password file:

```bash
sudo chmod 600 \
  ~/docker/mosquitto/config/passwordfile
```

## Final Permission Model

```text
Directories
   755

mosquitto.conf
   644

passwordfile
   600

Ownership
   1883:1883
```

## Result

Mosquitto successfully started and opened listeners on:

```text
1883
9001
```

The logs confirmed a working broker state.

## Engineering Lesson

> **Container bind mounts still obey host filesystem permissions.**

A correct Docker Compose file does not override Linux file ownership requirements.

